---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import '../styles/article.css';

const TITLE = 'Privacy Policy | ReflexLab';
const DESCRIPTION =
  'Privacy policy for ReflexLab. Your test results stay in your browser and never reach a server. What gets stored, what analytics collect, and how to delete it.';

// Bump this by hand whenever the policy text changes, and only then. Deriving
// it from the build date would stamp a fresh revision date on every deploy,
// which is exactly the claim a "last updated" line must not make falsely.
const LAST_UPDATED = '21 August 2026';
---

<BaseLayout title={TITLE} description={DESCRIPTION} path="/privacy" topbarLabel="Privacy">
  <article class="article-container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <span class="breadcrumbs-separator">/</span>
        <span class="breadcrumbs-current">Privacy Policy</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="article-title">Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="article-subtitle">How ReflexLab handles your data.</p>
      <p class="article-updated">Last updated {LAST_UPDATED}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="article-content">
      <p class="article-intro">
        Your scores stay on your device. ReflexLab has no accounts, no sign-ups, and no server that
        receives your results. Two things do involve a third party: Google Analytics measures
        traffic, and our host logs the requests it serves. Both are described below.
      </p>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>What ReflexLab Stores</h2>
        <p>
          Every test writes to your browser's localStorage under keys beginning with{' '}
          <code>reflexlab:</code>. For each test on the site, that covers:
        </p>
        <ul class="article-list">
          <li>Your personal best, and the settings you set it under</li>
          <li>Your recent run history, with a timestamp on each run</li>
          <li>Which rank tiers or milestones you have reached</li>
          <li>Your settings for that test, such as duration, delay, input method, and sound</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          None of it is transmitted. It stays in the browser profile that wrote it, which also means
          your scores will not follow you to another browser or another device. If you used
          ReflexLab before its rebuild, you may still hold keys beginning{' '}
          <code>reactionlab_</code> or <code>cpslab_</code> from the earlier version of the site. Those
          were imported once so your old personal bests survived, and the Reset button erases them along
          with everything else.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>No Accounts or Sign-Ups</h2>
        <p>
          ReflexLab never asks you to create an account, give an email address, or sign in. Open a
          test and take it. We hold no personal information about you because we never collect any.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Deleting Your Data</h2>
        <p>
          Each test has its own Reset button in the toolbar above the test area. It clears that
          test's personal best, run history, trend graph, and achieved ranks, and it leaves your
          settings in place so you do not have to rebuild them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Reset acts on the test you are looking at. Clearing your CPS data leaves your reaction
          time scores untouched. To erase every test at once, clear this site's data through your
          browser settings.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Cookies</h2>
        <p>
          ReflexLab sets no cookies of its own. Your scores and settings live in localStorage, which
          is separate from cookies and never travels with a network request. Google Analytics does
          set cookies, described next.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Analytics</h2>
        <p>
          ReflexLab uses Google Analytics to count page views and see which tests people use. It
          collects the standard set: the pages you visit, your approximate location down to country
          or region, your device type, and your browser. It never receives your scores, which stay
          in localStorage and are never read by the analytics script.
        </p>
        <p>
          The script loads from <code>googletagmanager.com</code> and sets cookies that tell a repeat
          visit from a new one. These cookies carry no personally identifiable information. To opt out,
          install Google's browser add-on, block the domain with an extension, or turn off cookies for
          this site.
        </p>
        <p>
          For more information, see{' '}
          <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
            Google's Privacy Policy</a
          >.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Hosting</h2>
        <p>
          Vercel hosts ReflexLab. Like any web host, its servers log the requests they serve, and
          those logs include your IP address, the page you asked for, and your user agent. We do not
          use them to build a profile of you or link them to anything else.
        </p>
        <p>
          See{' '}
          <a
            href="https://vercel.com/legal/privacy-policy"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer">Vercel's Privacy Policy</a
          > for how they handle that data.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>What We Do Not Do</h2>
        <p>
          We do not ask for your name or email address, sell or share your data, or track you across
          other websites. Nothing you do inside a test reaches us.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="article-section">
        <h2>Changes to This Policy</h2>
        <p>
          When what the site does changes, this page changes with it, and the date at the top of the
          page moves to match. Anything material gets written here rather than announced elsewhere.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>

  <section class="disclaimer-section">
    <p class="disclaimer">
      <strong>Disclaimer:</strong> ReflexLab's tests are built for entertainment and personal tracking.
      Your device, browser, input hardware, and display refresh rate all shape the numbers you see. For
      clinical or research measurement, use calibrated laboratory equipment.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .article-updated {
    margin-block-start: var(--space-sm);
    font-size: var(--text-label-size);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
  }

  .disclaimer-section {
    padding: var(--space-xl) 0 0;
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--line-subtle);
  }

  .disclaimer {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin-inline: auto;
    font-size: var(--text-label-size);
    color: var(--ink-muted);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .article-content code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    color: var(--ink-primary);
    background: var(--panel-02);
    border: 1px solid var(--line-subtle);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
  }
</style>
